{
  "vulnerable_signal": "cur_bit (= bit_idx_valid ? scalar[bit_idx[3:0]] : 1'b0) gating the state <= S_ADD transition and dp_op_is_add/dp_op_start assertion inside the S_DOUBLE case of scalar_mult_ctrl.v",
  "vulnerable_states": "S_ADD (state == 3'd2) is entered from S_DOUBLE only when cur_bit == 1 (if (cur_bit) begin ... state <= S_ADD; end); when cur_bit == 0 the FSM instead takes the else branch and jumps directly from S_DOUBLE to S_NEXT_BIT (state == 3'd3), completely skipping the S_ADD state and its 4-cycle field_datapath ADD operation for that bit index.",
  "timing_dependency_description": "scalar_mult_ctrl.v always issues one DOUBLE operation per bit index (16 total, bit 15 down to bit 0), each of which occupies the S_DOUBLE state for 3 clock cycles before field_datapath.v's op_done pulses (DBL_CYCLES = 3), followed by exactly 1 cycle spent in S_NEXT_BIT bookkeeping. In addition, for every bit index where cur_bit == 1, the FSM transitions into S_ADD and occupies that state for 4 clock cycles before op_done pulses (ADD_CYCLES = 4, field_datapath.v); for bit indices where cur_bit == 0, S_ADD is skipped entirely and no extra cycles are spent. Cross-checking against an independent iverilog/vvp simulation of the shipped scalar_mult_ctrl.v and field_datapath.v (rather than an analytically hand-derived count) confirms that the total cycle_count latched at done for a full 16-bit run follows cycle_count = 97 + 6*popcount(scalar): running the RTL for scalar=1 (Hamming weight 1) yields cycle_count = 103, and for scalar=65535 (Hamming weight 16) yields cycle_count = 193, both consistent with this linear model (97 + 6*1 = 103; 97 + 6*16 = 193). This is an exact linear function of the Hamming weight of the secret scalar (the number of 1-bits), independent of the scalar's numeric magnitude or bit positions -- two scalars with the same Hamming weight but very different numeric values (e.g. 0x0001 and 0x8000) produce identical cycle_count and identical state-sequence lengths, while two scalars with different Hamming weight always differ in cycle_count by a multiple of 6. An attacker who can observe total run latency (via power trace, EM trace, or direct cycle counting) for repeated operations on the same secret scalar, or even a single precise timing measurement, can recover popcount(scalar) exactly, and by observing the state trace (or finer-grained per-bit timing within a single run, e.g. via power analysis distinguishing the 4-cycle DOUBLE-only bit period from the 8-cycle DOUBLE+ADD bit period) can recover every individual bit of the scalar in sequence from bit 15 down to bit 0, fully breaking scalar secrecy.",
  "remediation_description": "Eliminate the scalar-dependent branch in S_DOUBLE by making the S_DOUBLE -> S_ADD transition and the ADD operation issue unconditional for every bit index, regardless of cur_bit. Every bit index then always executes DOUBLE followed by ADD followed by S_NEXT_BIT, giving every scalar an identical total cycle_count and identical S_IDLE->S_DOUBLE->S_ADD->S_NEXT_BIT state sequence repeated 16 times, independent of the scalar value. To keep the computed result correct, the ADD result (dp_out_x/dp_out_y) is committed into the real accumulator registers (acc_x, acc_y) only when cur_bit == 1; when cur_bit == 0 the same ADD result is instead written into a pair of dummy/discarded registers (acc_x_dummy, acc_y_dummy) that are never read back into the accumulator or into result_x/result_y, so the mathematical result of the scalar multiplication is unaffected by the always-executed ADD. This is implemented with a 2:1 multiplexer on the accumulator-commit path in the (now unconditional) S_ADD completion logic, selected by the same cur_bit signal that previously gated the FSM branch, so the data path -- not the control path -- carries the scalar dependency, and control-flow/timing is fully equalized.",
  "remediation_rtl_sketch": "In S_DOUBLE, remove the `if (cur_bit) ... else ...` branch and always do: dp_op_start <= 1'b1; dp_op_is_add <= 1'b1; dp_in_x <= dp_out_x; dp_in_y <= dp_out_y; dp_add_x <= base_x; dp_add_y <= base_y; state <= S_ADD; (unconditionally, for every bit index). Add two new registers acc_x_dummy, acc_y_dummy (reset to 64'd0 alongside acc_x/acc_y). In S_ADD, when dp_op_done fires, replace `acc_x <= dp_out_x; acc_y <= dp_out_y;` with a mux keyed on the latched cur_bit for that bit index: `if (cur_bit_latched) begin acc_x <= dp_out_x; acc_y <= dp_out_y; end else begin acc_x_dummy <= dp_out_x; acc_y_dummy <= dp_out_y; end` (cur_bit_latched being cur_bit sampled when S_ADD was entered, since bit_idx has not yet advanced). state <= S_NEXT_BIT; unconditionally in both cases with the same 1-cycle latency. No other timing-affecting branches remain scalar-dependent; DOUBLE and ADD retain their existing fixed DBL_CYCLES/ADD_CYCLES latencies in field_datapath.v, which is otherwise unmodified.",
  "preserves_correct_output": true,
  "cycle_count_range": {
    "min": 97,
    "max": 193
  },
  "cycle_counts_by_scalar": [
    { "scalar": 0, "cycle_count": 97 },
    { "scalar": 1, "cycle_count": 103 },
    { "scalar": 85, "cycle_count": 121 },
    { "scalar": 3855, "cycle_count": 145 },
    { "scalar": 32767, "cycle_count": 187 },
    { "scalar": 65535, "cycle_count": 193 }
  ]
}
